/*!
    \page get-and-install-qt.html

    \title Get and Install Qt
    \brief Overview of the different ways to download and install Qt.

    You can install Qt on your computer in different ways. The table below outlines each
    method and when it is most useful.

    \table
      \header
        \li Method
        \li Description
        \li When to use
      \row
        \li \l{Get and Install Qt with \QOI}{\QOI (GUI)}
        \li A graphical installer that lets you choose Qt versions, modules, and tools
            interactively. In most cases, this is the best way to install Qt.
        \li
          \list
            \li You're new to Qt.
            \li You prefer a visual interface.
            \li You're installing Qt on a desktop computer.
          \endlist
      \row
        \li \l{Get and Install Qt with Command Line Interface}{\QOI (command line)}
        \li Allows unattended installation using scripts or automation tools.
        \li
          \list
            \li You need to install Qt on multiple machines.
            \li You're setting up CI/CD pipelines.
            \li You want to automate the installation without user interaction.
          \endlist
      \row
        \li \l{Building Qt Sources}{Build from sources}
        \li Download and compile Qt manually from source code.
        \li
          \list
            \li You need a custom build (for example, with patches or debug options).
            \li Your platform isn't supported by prebuilt packages.
            \li You're contributing to Qt or debugging its internals.
          \endlist
      \row
        \li Package managers:
          \list
            \li \l{Qt for macOS - Alternative ways of installing Qt}{macOS}
            \li \l{Qt for Linux - Alternative ways of installing Qt}{Linux}
          \endlist
        \li Allows you to install a recent version of Qt without requiring a Qt account.
        \li
          \list
            \li Lightweight and good for automation.
            \li You need to install Qt as a dependency for your applications, but don't plan
            to do development work with Qt yourself.
            \li You don't need special or commercial-only modules.
          \endlist
    \endtable

After you have installed Qt, you can add, update, and remove components with \QMT
(included in every Qt installation). For more information, see
\l{Add, update, and remove installed Qt components}{Add, update, and remove components}.

*/
